  "summary": "Recent work adds imaging preprocessing tools, neuron-level metabolic models, and callosal genetics to disease-linked studies of brain architecture.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational integration of heterogeneous brain data, from imaging to genome-scale models, to relate structural and molecular variation to disease phenotypes. TRAECR standardizes PET preprocessing for statistical modeling through registration, MRI co-registration, and COMBAT/RAVEL harmonization. A TBI preprint pursues phenotype discovery across heterogeneous multi-site segmentations, motivated by the limits of Glasgow Coma Scale outcomes. On the molecular side, iNeuron-GEM, a curated genome-scale metabolic network for human neurons, is coupled with snRNA-seq from ROSMAP and SEA-AD to resolve neuronal subtype-specific metabolic changes in neurodegenerative and neuropsychiatric disease. A GWAS meta-analysis of 46,685 European individuals, using AI-extracted corpus callosum morphometry, maps the genetic architecture of callosal subregions and their neuropsychiatric associations. Notable is the pairing of harmonized imaging pipelines with cell-type-resolved metabolic and genetic models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-10"
}
